kasturi@vu-vlsi.ee.vill.edu (Srinivasa M. Kasturi - EE8460 Spring 1991) writes:
>Are commercial Terminal Emulation packages insulated from whichever kind of
>network used.  That is do the emulation packages generally use specific 
>connectivity options like Sockets to send in the key strokes, etc. they trap.  
>In which case how would one go about using DCE-RPC based Integrity or Privacy
>kinds of connections with emulators already purchased.

Most communications products expect to open their connections
their own way.  Certainly, a DCE RPC application has to be built
to use RPC; it is not something that can be slapped on.

Your best bet is probably to re-think the problem.
If you must, you could write an RPC application that accepts input
from socket, say, and ships the bytes via RPC.  The terminal emulator
would be told to open a connection that turns out to be on the same machine.
But this a round-about approach.
