I've had varargs problems with DEC's cc.  I've had no problems with
gcc 2.5.5 and greater PROVIDED that you insert the following magic
in your compilation lines/Makefiles:

-I<root_of_gcc_install_tree>/lib/gcc-lib/alpha-dec-osf1.3/<gcc_vers>/include

Just to make this concrete, for me this is:

-I/usr/ndim/local/lib/gcc-lib/alpha-dec-osf1.3/2.5.7/include

For some odd reason which I have not yet had time to figure out, gcc
either does not do this by default or puts this directory after all
the standard ones on its paths.  In any case, this is a Bad Thing,
since it causes the /usr/include version of varargs.h (or stdargs.h,
depending on how you're doing your stuff) to be found before gcc's.
If you put the above -I in your calls to gcc, your varargs code will
magically work.

I *strongly* suggest folks doing serious development on alphas go out
and get gcc 2.5.7 (NOT 2.5.8 -- it gets fatal internal errors trying
to compile simple things) and gdb 4.12, minimally.  Even if you like
the DEC cc's optimizer better, the GNU stuff is still a much better
environment for debugging (as in, it works).  One word of caution,
however: do NOT use -ggdb if you intend to use pixie, as it puts
things in the symbol table that confuses prof.

Hope this helps.

Cheers,
				-- Sean
