James Litchfield - SunSoft Western Area OS Person <jiml@bardolph.West.Sun.COM> wrote:
>I have been told by the Transarc sales people that any Unix based *DCE* rpc
>app needs to be run (and developed) in the context of a DCE cell (i.e., you
>must have a functioning cell which requires (at a minimum) a CDS server and a
>Security Server). CDS is needed to locate the system the server portion of the
>rpc app resides on and the security server is used for authentication.

This is not *strictly* true.  If your app don't use the security or
rpc_ns_* calls (and just uses string bindings for server rendezvous),
you don't need a security or name server running.  Your functionality
is somewhat limited though.

You can also run a non-replicated security server without CDS; it will
periodically complain about not being able to register itself in the
name service, but that isn't fatal.  Note that this configuration is
most certainly "not supported" (i.e., if you do it, you're on your
own).

>It appears that the list you were provided is the minimum one would logically 
>need to purchase to do such development. 

This, however, is true except in very limited circumstances.

To help you get started, HP sells a "starter kit" bundle which
contains software, documentation, and licenses for a five-node cell at
a discount.

