In article <2n9uu4$nf6@reznor.larc.nasa.gov>, cordner@hercules.larc.nasa.gov (Dave Cordner) writes:
|> We at NASA Langley are looking at distributed file systems for our heterogeneous
|> workstation/supercomputing environment.  AFS seems like it supports a lot of
|> platforms at this point in time but it looks as if DFS is going to be a more
|> flexible and better implimentation of a distributed file system.  Are there any
|> suggestions or words of wisdom about which direction to start out in?  It seems
|> like DFS is just getting under way (new unavailable?) but I am concerned about 
|> manpower requirements to migrate a significant number of machines on the center 
|> from AFS to DFS in a couple of years.  "People" are our least available resource 
|> here.
|> 
|> Any thoughts?

I think DFS is a better choice.  However, it is important to know
that though DFS is available from vendors like IBM (for AIX DCE),
it is (in my view) not a very robust product yet. This doesn't
mean that you can't use it.   It just means that you will have to
do some extra work to provide a production quality DFS. 


  The following are some of the problems I faced with DFS on AIX.

1) The DCE default user set does not completely match with AIX user id set.
   For example, standard user names like bin, mail have different default
   id numbers in DCE and AIX.  This may lead to some problems in the future.
   I think IBM is planning to fix this soon.

2) Intercell DFS setup is not totally secure.  All the foreign users
   in one cell are authenticated using one 'special unix id' (
   which was established during intercell setup).  Hence, if one
   foreign user creates a file in the local cell, it is accessible by
   another foreign user.  IBM says this shouldn't happen.  But it happened.
 
3) The DFS backup mechanisms do not provide a 'true' automated scheduled
   backups to tapes.  Kind of hard for sys admin folks to come out in the
   middle of the night and push return key when it interactive butc (backup 
   program) program wants to start the backup. 

4) Considering the fact that DFS is so new, to get quality technical support 
   you have to somehow get in touch with the developers. I can confidently say
   problem resolution takes time.  

5) I don't know about the availability of DFS from HP, Sun or other vendors.
   I heard that they also planning to ship DFS very soon (since, I guess, they
   want don't want to die :-) 

6) There may be problems in migrating the existing software (applications as
   opposed to files) to DFS.  For example, Oracle or Sybase servers need 
   DFS authentication to write to the log files (or whatever) if they (log
   files) are located in DFS.  

7) If the users filesystems (home directories) are in DFS,  they have
   to authenticate to DCE to access their home directories and 
   to authenticate to DCE they need to have a command prompt in their
   home directory.  Some kind of catch 22.   

Please note that most of these problems can be overcome with a little
bit of effort (writing some scripts, resigning from the jobs :-) etc.) from
the sysadmin folks.  It is a moot matter whether you want to go for it.  

6) On the positive side, DFS is an excellent product and it smoothens out
   a lot of file system sharing issues in a distributed environment.  Provides
   a very fine control for the limiting the access to files, provides 
   replication of critical systems, comes with quick recovery mechanisms,
   and it is also very easy to move filesystems transparently between the
   machines across a network without the users noticing any change; It is also
   well integrated into DCE.  Provides a common enterprise wide hierarchy 
   of files and directories.  Along with DFS on IBM mainframes, it sure
   provides a way to downsizing of legacies.


Disclaimer: The above opinions are entirely mine and I do not represent any
            body or any organization though I work for somebody in some 
            organization.
						Kumar Kakumanu
						 
 



                                 



