> I am pretty sure I am controlling for all extraneous factors. My
> measurement code runs 100 times, only referencing an array where it
> sticks the running time. All the code fits in the I-cache. I'm not
> getting interrupts (the whole measurement takes about 10 uS, and the
> effect of interrupts is real obvious when they do occur.)  The whole
> shebang (code & data) fits in the off-chip B-cache, and my flush
> vector should not flush the register load/store areas out of there.

Well, I controlled them all except one. In order that I could call
the context switch code from a C program, I replaced the loads and
stores to all the miscellaneous registers, with loads and stores to
$31 (the one which reads as zero). It turns out that the instruction

    LDQ $31,address

is a NOP - it never actually references any memory. I am quite
surprised that it actually does this - I'm sure on a MIPS or most
other RISCs it would do the load anyway, and just let the bits fall
off the end of the wire in the register file. So that explains
everything. It even sort of suggests that this might happen in the
manual.

In fact, it appears that the way Dirk Grunwald originally wrote the
AweSime context switcher is the fastest way possible - loads and
stores interleaved.

