In article <2l5rfl$gig@agate.berkeley.edu>,
John Ousterhout <ouster@sprite.Berkeley.EDU> wrote:
>Does anyone know of a document that describes the exact look and
>feel of the official Motif widgets, including all of their key and
>mouse bindings?  One of my goals for Tk 4.0 is to make Tk conform
>much more closely to the Motif look and feel (particularly the feel),
>but I'm having trouble finding a good description of the Motif
>widgets (I've got the OSF Motif Style Guide but it isn't a lot
>of help).

(I've added comp.human-factors an comp.unix.osf.misc to the groups
as someone there may know the answer.)

The style guide is for application programmers using the Motif
toolkit, of course... not for the people seeking to write a
look-and-feel compatible toolkit, which is why its not that useful to
you.  I seem to recall that Motif actually implements some IBM
user-interface specs... anybody know more about this and where to get
the definitive document?

But this begs the question of whether 100% look-and-feel compatibility
is desireable... Motif is not perfect, and I imagine some improvements
could be made.  For example, I still don't understand why Motif
pop-downs don't pop back up when you click on the title... I've
delibarately done some informal experiments by observing people who
had never touched a Motif application before using X Mosaic, and
almost invariably the first thing they did to try to get rid of a
pop-down was to click on the title.  Didn't the people who designed
this stuff do any user testing?  It also seems conceptually bad to
force the user to click on an otherwise non-active component of the
interface (or some component that would have a different action
associated with it) to cause the action of popping a pop-down back up.

One of the areas where Tk seems to currently differ from Motif and
where I prefer the Motif way is the behavior of scrollbars in many
widgets...  Tk scrollbars scroll the end of a document to the top of
the screen, leaving a full blank screen at the bottom of each document
(and causing the elevator to disappear at the bottom of the
scrollbar.)  Is this a bug or a feature?  If it's a feature I fail to
see the rationale.

