> I have a group of ALpha OSF/1 machines here running NIS for sharing the 
> passwd and group files, and need an easy way of having root change the 
> users passwords when they forget them. There are over 5000 users in the
> passwd file, so doing a makedbm on it takes a long time, so zeroing out
> the passwd entry, doing a make, and then a yppasswd(1) to actuallly change
> the entry takes too much time. Has anyone written or seen anything that
> will let root change the yppasswd entry without knowing the old passwd?


1. There is NO WAY to change a password using the RPC interface (used
   by yppasswd), without knowing the current password.  That's just how
   the RPC interface works.  No way around it.

2. If you really want *fast* password changing, then you will need to
   write some C code (or Perl) to directly manipulate the DBM files
   that the NIS maps are stashed in.  It's not hard to do that, especially
   in Perl.

