maxims@uclink.berkeley.edu (Maxim Spivak) writes:

>Hello all,

>First of all please forgive me if the questions here are 'stupid', I'm 
>just starting in this area...

>I'm a recent convert to the Unix world and am using Linux on a PC.
>My understanding of the whole 'windowing' issue is vague, but I
>think I got it correctly that OCF/motif is a GUI implementation that
>runs on top of XWindows and that DCE is a window environment/
>shell/interface. 

>Is this correct? Where can I get more info about this? Is DCE 
>available already. Does it exist for Linux or does it need to be
>ported? What are the motif libraries? How do I know if I have them?
>Please feel free to expand on these questions, I'm not sure I'm
>asking the right things...


You are approximately correct about Motif, which is a GUI style,
and a widget set and window manager that implements that style
for the X Window System.

DCE, on the other hand, has nothing to do with windowing. 
It is a set of facilities to support distributed computing:
 a remote procedure call mechanism, a directory service, 
 a security system, a time service, and a distributed file service.

Both Motif and DCE are products of OSF and require royalties.
Neither one is available for free.  Motif is avaiable as a commercial
product on Linux, and there is an effort to build a work-alike product.
DCE is not available on Linux, though I am tempted to start an
effort to build such a thing.
