We have several alpha osf/1 machines here on campus, and as soon as I put
one up live, the complaints started rolling in about its NFS performance
while serving PCNFS machines. After satisfying myself that the alpha
concerned (A 3000/600 using thickwire/AUI connection) was idling along
at only 5% or less utilisation, and very little IO, I looked at the
network statistics with netstat -i 2, and lo and behold, the sucker is
getting collisions by the bucketful.

Its very peaky, but in the order of 7 collisions from 35 packets over 2 
seconds. Only rarely does it go to 0. Under load its even worse. 30%
collisions when transferring a large (~30MB) file between the alphas
(Any one, they all do it. 3000/300's as well) and another machine (VAX, 
RS6k etc). But NFS is the one where the performance is getting hit.

Note that the ethernet isn't loaded at all. We are lucky if thet segment/
subnet gets above 10%. Has anyone else seen huge collision counts like
this with alphas?

Oh. BTW. The same file to/from a mips box (The ones we are upgrading 
from :) gets almost 0 collisions with a comparable (But not equal) packet
rate.


