Subject: Strange behaviour of Alpha Floating Point Load/Stores wrt cache misses
Message-ID: <TLB.94Mar2215356@chardonnay.harvard.edu>
Sender: usenet@das.harvard.edu
Organization: Harvard University
Date: Thu, 3 Mar 1994 02:53:56 GMT
Lines: 175


This one really baffles me. I measure very different performance of
load and store instructions, between when I do a LDQ/STQ (load/store
quadword = 8 bytes), and LDT/SDT (load/store IEEE FP double = 8
bytes), especially when cache misses are involved. These are the same
size memory transfers, done in the same order, and I would have
expected them to perform identically. The architecture manual claims
that LDT/SDT do not do any FP work such as normalizing; they just
transfer bits between the register and memory.

[This came up because I was interested in the register save/restore
code involved in a context switch, and whether it could be optimized
by interleaving loads & stores to different cache lines, given the
Alpha's load/store buffers. It may look wierd because I messed around
with the order of load/saves to try and hit the right cache lines
early].

Look at the attached assembler file below (adapted from the AweSime
threads library). It loads and saves the registers (I changed it to
use registers $31 & $f1 for everything, so I could call it normally)

I called it from code that makes almost no (like, 1, contrived not to
alias with anything) memory references between calls, except every
fifth time it loads a 8K-long vector, to flush the cache. Something
like:

  for (i=0; i<100; i++) {
    if (i%5==0) flushData();
    timing[i] = magicSwitchTo1(area1,area2);
  }

If the #if is 1, (so it does integer and FP transfers) then a typical
timing sequence (times in clock cycles, timing with RPCC) is:

 ...
 Time = 83 (with flush)
 Time = 54 (no flush)
 Time = 66 (no flush)
 Time = 66 (no flush)
 Time = 66 (no flush)
 Time = 83 (with flush)
 Time = 54 (no flush)
 Time = 66 (no flush)
 Time = 66 (no flush)
 Time = 66 (no flush)
 ...

If the #if is 0, (integer transfers only) then I get:

 ...
 Time = 50 (with flush)
 Time = 50 (no flush)
 Time = 61 (no flush)
 Time = 61 (no flush)
 Time = 61 (no flush)
 Time = 50 (with flush)
 Time = 50 (no flush)
 Time = 61 (no flush)
 Time = 61 (no flush)
 Time = 61 (no flush)
 ...

This is totally repeatable. Email me for my complete code if you want
to play with it.

This makes absolutely no sense at all to me. Does anyone know why this
might be happening? In particular:

  - Why are FP and integer different?

  - Why do I get SHORTER times with integer only AFTER flushing the cache?

  - Why is the second time after flushing the cache short, but the
    third time longer? Is this because of something about hits on dirty
    (writeback-pending) processor cache lines?

I note that reordering the first few instructions makes a big
difference (as you might expect - you win if you can get both memory
load registers reading in cache lines simultaneously). I got the total
time down from 120 clocks to 83 clocks in the mixed integer/FP case by
putting the right things in the first 5 instructions.

I am pretty sure I am controlling for all extraneous factors. My
measurement code runs 100 times, only referencing an array where it
sticks the running time. All the code fits in the I-cache. I'm not
getting interrupts (the whole measurement takes about 10 uS, and the
effect of interrupts is real obvious when they do occur.)  The whole
shebang (code & data) fits in the off-chip B-cache, and my flush
vector should not flush the register load/store areas out of there.

Any explanations? [Whatever happened to nice, predictable CPUs, like
the 6502, where you could count your clock cycles yourself?]

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/* save registers from $16, load from $17 */
magicSwitchTo1:	
	.frame $30,0,$26,0
	.prologue 0

	rpcc $3

	stq	$31,32($16)
	stq	$31,104($16)
	ldq	$31,32($17)
	ldq	$31,152($17)
	
	stq	$31,40($16)
	ldq	$31,40($17)
	stq	$31,48($16)
	ldq	$31,48($17)
	stq	$31,56($16)
	ldq	$31,56($17)
	stq	$31,64($16)
	ldq	$31,64($17)
	stq	$31,72($16)
	ldq	$31,72($17)
	stq	$31,80($16)
	ldq	$31,80($17)
	stq	$31,88($16)
	ldq	$31,88($17)
	stq	$31,96($16)
	ldq	$31,96($17)

#if 1
	ldq	$31,104($17)
	stq	$31,112($16)
	ldq	$31,112($17)
	stq	$31,120($16)
	ldq	$31,120($17)
	stq	$31,128($16)
	ldq	$31,128($17)
	stq	$31,136($16)
	ldq	$31,136($17)
	stq	$31,144($16)
	ldq	$31,144($17)
	stq	$31,152($16)
#else
	ldt	$f1,104($17)
	stt	$f1,112($16)
	ldt	$f1,112($17)
	stt	$f1,120($16)
	ldt	$f1,120($17)
	stt	$f1,128($16)
	ldt	$f1,128($17)
	stt	$f1,136($16)
	ldt	$f1,136($17)
	stt	$f1,144($16)
	ldt	$f1,144($17)
	stt	$f1,152($16)
#endif
	stq	$31,24($16)
	ldq	$31,24($17)
	stq	$31,0($16)
	stq	$31,8($16)
	ldq	$31,8($17)
	ldq	$31,0($17)

	rpcc $1
	
	sll $3,32,$2
	addq $3,$2,$3
	srl $3,32,$3
	
	sll $1,32,$2
	addq $1,$2,$1
	srl $1,32,$1
	subq $1,$3,$0
	
	ret $31,($26),1

